Title: The Tell-Tale Heart

Credit: Written by

Author: Edgar Allan Poe

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

Notes: Rendered as gothic period live-action, candlelit interiors, high contrast shadow.

FADE IN:

INT. CONFESSION ROOM - NIGHT

A bare room lit by one guttering candle. THE NARRATOR — a gaunt man of no fixed age, dark hollow eyes, plain dark coat buttoned to the throat — sits rigid in a hard chair, facing camera as though facing an unseen questioner.

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

His hands do not move. His eyes do not blink.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
I think it was his eye! Yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
And so, by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

EXT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - NIGHT

An old, narrow house leans against the dark. Shutters closed fast. No light within.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

THE OLD MAN — elderly, thin white hair, a pale blue eye filmed over with a cloud — sleeps beneath heavy blankets. A latch turns. The door creaks open by inches. THE NARRATOR's head appears in the gap, a shuttered dark lantern held before him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently.

The narrator's head inches further into the room, achingly slow.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

He crouches at the bedside, lantern closed tight. The old man's eye stays shut.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed. It was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

Morning light. THE NARRATOR stands in the doorway, hearty, calling into the room.

NARRATOR
Good morning to you. And how did you pass the night?

The OLD MAN, unsuspecting, waves a hand from the bed.

INT. CONFESSION ROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR leans forward in his chair, eyes bright.

NARRATOR
So you see he would have been a very profound old man indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Deep night. The door creaks open once more — the eighth night. THE NARRATOR's hand trembles on the lantern's tin fastening.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

His thumb slips. A faint metallic click. THE OLD MAN springs upright in the dark.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

THE NARRATOR freezes, statue-still, one hand still on the lantern.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

A long, low groan rises from the bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. Many a night, just at midnight, it has welled up from my own bosom. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him — although I chuckled at heart.

THE NARRATOR's thumb eases open a hairline crack in the lantern. A single thread of light crawls across the dark and settles on the OLD MAN's open, filmed blue eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

A low, dull, quick thudding sound rises — a watch wrapped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It was the beating of the old man's heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

The thudding grows louder. THE NARRATOR's hand shakes; the lantern beam trembles on the wall.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder — louder every moment! And now a new anxiety seized me — the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

THE NARRATOR throws open the lantern with a yell and lunges into the room.

He drags the OLD MAN to the floor and pulls the heavy bed down over him. A single shriek, cut short.

Silence, but for a muffled beating beneath the bedclothes, fading.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This did not vex me. At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

THE NARRATOR lifts the bed away. He presses a hand flat to the old man's chest and holds it there.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Later — still dark. Three loose floor planks lie stacked to one side. THE NARRATOR kneels, working with a knife, methodical, unhurried. A washtub sits nearby, dark and full.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse.

He fits the last remains between the scantling and lowers the boards back into place, pressing each seam smooth with his palm.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye could have detected any thing wrong. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A distant clock strikes four.

INT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - NIGHT

A knock sounds at the street door. THE NARRATOR crosses unhurried, candle in hand, and opens it wide, smiling.

Three police officers stand on the step — OFFICER MORAN, broad and grey-whiskered; OFFICER DUFFY; OFFICER KELLY, younger, watchful.

OFFICER MORAN
A shriek was heard by a neighbour in the night. We've been sent to search the premises.

NARRATOR
Come in, gentlemen. The shriek was my own — in a dream. The old man is absent, in the country.

He leads them through the house, gesturing broadly at each door.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Search well, search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR ushers the officers into the bedchamber and shows them the old man's undisturbed treasures in a cabinet.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues — while I myself placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

He sets his own chair squarely over the mended planks and sits. The officers settle, at ease.

OFFICER DUFFY
A fine old house, this.

NARRATOR
It has stood longer than I have.

They chat a while of ordinary things — the weather, the street, the hour. THE NARRATOR answers cheerily. Then, faintly, a low, dull, quick sound.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears. But still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct.

He glances down at the floorboards beneath his own boots. The sound is not in his ears.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
No doubt I now grew very pale — but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice.

He rises, gesturing broadly, voice pitched high, arguing some trifle. The officers only smile and nod. The sound grows.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations. But the noise steadily increased.

He paces, boots striking the boards. The thudding swells beneath the floor, louder with each step.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Why would they not be gone? I foamed — I raved — I swore! I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards.

He seizes his chair and grates it hard across the planks. The sound roars up, filling the room. The officers go on smiling, chatting — untroubled.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not? They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

His face contorts. He can bear it no longer.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed! Tear up the planks!—here, here!—it is the beating of his hideous heart!

He drops to his knees and claws at the floor. The officers freeze, staring.

INT. CONFESSION ROOM - NIGHT

Back in the bare candlelit room. THE NARRATOR sits exactly as before, hands now trembling on his knees, breath unsteady, the confession finished.

NARRATOR
It is the beating of his hideous heart.

The candle gutters. His eyes stare past camera into the dark.

FADE OUT.

THE END
